"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[46053],{746053:e=>{e.exports='# Spark Declarative Pipelines Testing with TestPipeline\n\nUse pytest with the **TestPipeline API** for Spark Declarative Pipelines code. This applies to all pipeline decorator types: `@dp.materialized_view`, `@dp.table` (streaming tables), `@dp.streaming_table`, and `@dp.view`.\n\n## Key Rules\n\n- Import the mandatory `test_spark` fixture for catalog isolation.\n- Mock input data by creating tables through `test_spark.sql()` with sample rows matching the source schema.\n- For streaming tables (`@dp.table` with `readStream`), mock input using regular tables — TestPipeline handles the streaming read automatically.\n  `test_pipeline.run()` returns a **`TestUpdateStatus`** object with fields `update_id`, `error_message`, `error_class`, and `event_log_table_name`, plus a derived `is_success` property. It does **not** return query results or column data.\n- Use `test_pipeline.run(test_spark, set([...]))` for targeted refreshes of specific datasets.\n- Use `test_pipeline.run(test_spark)` when verifying full dependency chains.\n- Always check `status.is_success` after a run to confirm the pipeline update succeeded before querying output tables.\n- After a successful run, read output tables via `test_spark.sql("SELECT * FROM ...")` to verify transformation results.\n- Assert the transformation semantics (filters, joins, aggregations, derived columns), not only row counts.\n- Use `test_spark` for table reads and writes inside tests.\n\n## Common Pattern — Materialized View\n\n```python\nfrom pyspark.sql import SparkSession\nimport pytest\nfrom pyspark.pipelines.testing import TestPipeline, test_spark\n\ntest_pipeline = TestPipeline.active()\n```\n\n## Common Pattern — Streaming Table (`@dp.table`)\n\nStreaming tables defined with `@dp.table` and `spark.readStream` are tested the same way. Mock the source as a regular table; TestPipeline converts the read to streaming internally.\n\n```python\nfrom pyspark.pipelines.testing import TestPipeline, test_spark\n\ntest_pipeline = TestPipeline.active()\n\ndef test_streaming_table_filters_correctly(test_spark):\n    # Mock the source table with regular rows\n    test_spark.sql("""\n        CREATE OR REPLACE TABLE test_catalog.test_schema.raw_events (\n            event_id STRING, event_type STRING, ts TIMESTAMP\n        )\n    """)\n    test_spark.sql("""\n        INSERT INTO test_catalog.test_schema.raw_events VALUES\n        (\'1\', \'click\', \'2025-01-01T00:00:00\'),\n        (\'2\', \'view\', \'2025-01-01T00:01:00\'),\n        (\'3\', \'click\', \'2025-01-01T00:02:00\')\n    """)\n\n    # Run only the streaming table target — returns TestUpdateStatus\n    status = test_pipeline.run(test_spark, set(["filtered_clicks"]))\n    assert status.is_success, f"Pipeline run failed: {status.error_message}"\n\n    # Query the output table to verify transformation results\n    result = test_spark.sql("SELECT * FROM test_catalog.test_schema.filtered_clicks")\n    assert result.count() == 2\n    assert all(row.event_type == "click" for row in result.collect())\n```\n'}}]);
